Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.32,0:00:03.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Impose the budget on the\Ncrowd, during training.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.68,0:00:07.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw a batch of snapshots at\Nany {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}, even without data.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.75,0:00:11.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Average their energy change\Nand square it: this penalty,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.55,0:00:15.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the crowd loss, vanishes\Nonly when the crowd balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.42,0:00:17.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But there is a trap.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.79,0:00:21.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even for a balanced crowd,\Na batch's average wobbles;
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.26,0:00:23.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}its square grows with the scatter.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.79,0:00:26.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So training also squeezes the scatter,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.29,0:00:30.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}nudging every snapshot toward\Nbalance: toward the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.27,0:00:33.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It showed up while\Nbuilding this experiment.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.17,0:00:37.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The fix: split the batch\Ninto two independent
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.21,0:00:39.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}juries and multiply their averages.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.87,0:00:44.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, the scatter drops out,\Nleaving only the crowd's imbalance.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.78,0:00:47.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.
